WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:11.219
One of the questions I get asked a lot is what screenwriting books should I buy?

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.379
What one is the best but I actually think that's the wrong question to ask?

00:00:16.779 --> 00:00:20.429
Because the answer I give is entirely dependent on one thing

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:26.070
What your weakness is as a writer because it's only by working on our weaknesses that we can improve

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:30.480
Let's say you struggle with character. They aren't believable or feel mechanical

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:34.230
Also just a different book than if your problem was structure or plot

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:35.040
so in this video

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:40.709
I want to break down some of my favorite screenwriting books and the reasons I'd recommend them and stay tuned because at the end I'll

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:44.279
Give my opinion for the best thing you can be reading as a screenwriter

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:49.410
The first is Sid fields screenplay often consider the bible of screenwriting books

00:00:49.559 --> 00:00:52.559
it's an in-depth analysis of 3x structure and a

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:57.539
Fantastic starting point for beginners or those who struggle with plot and structure now

00:00:57.539 --> 00:01:03.779
I've been noticing a paradigm shift away from 3x structure in the last few years particularly in the TV sphere

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:09.720
But this method is still dominant and as such every screenwriter should have a firm understanding of it

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:16.229
Even if they don't intend to use it similar to screenplay and one of my personal favorites is Robert McKee's story

00:01:16.229 --> 00:01:22.199
I usually recommend this as the second book to read after screenplay because they complement each other

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.000
It's a little less accessible

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.080
But the stuff in here is fantastic with greater expansion on three-act structure

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:30.130
some character work and useful

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:36.240
examples of how his techniques applied to films we know and love and then there's what's commonly referred to as the

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:43.140
People magazine of screenwriting Blake Snyder's save the cat if you want to write mainstream high concept

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.280
Blockbusters. This is the book for you

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:52.559
It's an entertaining read that introduces some interesting concepts that I still use like Pope in the pool or double mumbo-jumbo

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:57.810
If you don't know what those things are check this book out now, I want to get into some books

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:03.479
I'd recommend if you struggle with character character development and to some extent dialogue because

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:10.410
Dialogue is intrinsically linked to character often if you have trouble with dialogue it's rooted in a problem with the character

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:13.619
The first book is km. Wyland's creating character arc

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:20.249
this is one of my absolute favorites so much so that I did an entire video using her concepts and applied it to

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:21.630
Star Wars in it

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:27.659
You'll learn about the three different types of character arcs and how a character shapes the story not the other way around

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:34.890
It's also a great analysis of how your character changes over the course of the film or how they change the world around them

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:36.270
highly recommend

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:42.089
The second is Lagos agrees the art of dramatic writing now this is actually a book about playwriting

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:49.020
But a lot of the stuff in here is transferable for us screenwriters chiefly among them is the idea of the tri-dimensional

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:54.959
Character in which he argues that in order to understand how characters react in certain situations

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.630
We must first know three things about them. There's sociology physiology and psychology

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:07.049
So if you've ever gotten the note, oh, I don't think the character would do that or your characters aren't believable

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:14.160
I'd check out this book. Then there are the books. I consider outliers. The first is John troubies the anatomy of story

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:21.779
Which offers a different approach to storytelling that as a direct refutation of the traditional three act structure tired of McKee or field

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:26.999
This is the book for you offering new paradigm shifting techniques on how to approach story

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:34.679
I also love how truly uses multiple films as example throughout to highlight his tools and concepts then there's Christopher Vogler's the writer's

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.930
Journey, which looks at storytelling from a place of heroic. Myth a character goes into the unknown

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:43.379
Battles his or her demons and comes back a hero

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:49.019
The interesting thing about the writers journey is that it can fit in almost any of the affer mentioned structures

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.370
This book is also great

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:54.690
If you're stuck say there's a problem with your script and you just can't figure it out

00:03:54.850 --> 00:04:01.679
going through the steps of the writers journey can help pinpoint where your problem is and offer solutions on how to solve it last and

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:08.520
Certainly not least the best thing you can be reading to better yourself as a writer is to read other scripts

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.290
Read bad ones read good ones immerse yourself in other people's work

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:18.330
You'll start learning what you like what you don't pay seing tone and other things you can't learn from

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:22.798
Watching the movie or reading a book as someone who has read over a thousand scripts

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.870
I can honestly say that reading others has been one of the greatest learning periences

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:33.959
I'll leave a link below where you can find out how to access some of these scripts and I also suggest checking out the annual

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:38.280
Blacklist to give you a sense of what is out there and what is getting buzz?

00:04:38.319 --> 00:04:43.829
So that's my breakdown of my favorite screenwriting books. What are yours? Let me know in the comments below

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:51.479
I'd love your feedback. And as always thanks for watching. Don't forget to Like and subscribe and hit that Bell button. Keep on writing

